          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Spaces can be characterized by their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebras
            of functions (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality+-+table"} Isbell duality - table
          %p
            Using this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            one can define generalized spaces in terms of generalizations of their algebras of functions.
          %p The idea of noncommutative geometry is to encode everything about the geometry of a space algebraically and then allow all commutative function algebras to be generalized to possibly non-commutative algebras.
          %p
            More generally,
            %strong noncommutative geometry
            means replacing the space by some structure carried by an entity (or a collection of entities) living on that would-be space. The entity may be for example a function, vector bundle, coherent sheaf, a complex of sheaves and KK-theory class. Objects organize into
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebras"} operator algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reconstruction"} Reconstruction
            theorems are theorems on construction of a genuine (say topological) “underlying” space of such an entity or collection. Spectral theories are procedures (sometimes functors, often not) which recover some form of underlying space called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+%28geometry%29"} spectrum
          %p
            Under the process of forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+convolution+algebras"} groupoid convolution algebras
            a good bit of commutative but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            translates into noncommutative geometry. This is for instance the origin of the role of noncommutative geometry in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#connes_noncommutative_geometry Connes’ noncommutative geometry
          %p
            A particular and most prominent realization of the program of noncommutative geometry has been lead by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+geometry"} spectral
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            (see there for more).
          %p The central ingredients in Connes’ noncommutative geometry are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the idea to characterize a (noncommutative) space by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of global functions
                on that space; this approach has been occasionally considered earlier e.g. in the book of Semadeni on Banach algebras.
            %li
              %p There is a refined, quantized differential calculus where the differential is given by a commutator formula involving a Fredholm operator; the setup in which this is taken place involves cyclic cocycles discovered by Tsygan and Connes.
            %li
              %p
                the noncommutative analog of the structure of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
                with a spin structure in terms of generalized
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                acting on a representation space of the algebra
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            For that reason
            = succeed "\u2018" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Connes
            = succeed "." do
              %strong spectral geometry
          %ul
            %li Noncommutative measure spaces are represented by noncommutative von Neumann algebras.
          %p
            In noncommutative geometry various homotopical and (co)homological invariants were introduced by large amount of improvisation, similar to the beginnings of algebraic topology, but more recently there are few systematic approaches to homotopy theory emerging. See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+operator+algebras"} model structure on operator algebras
